---
// The third tool page. One URL, indexable, its own title and prose — people arrive here from
// a search for "reorder pdf pages", not from the landing page.
//
// The interactive part is one island. Everything above and below it is static, so the page is
// readable and its claim is checkable with JavaScript switched off.
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { STRIP_WITHDRAWN, WITHDRAWN_MARK } from "../components/strip-copy.js";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Reorder PDF pages — rearrange them on your own computer | Not Only PDF"
  description="Move pages around inside a PDF. The pages are rearranged in your browser and never uploaded, because there is no server to upload them to."
>
  <div class="read">
    <h1>Reorder PDF pages</h1>

    <p>
      Move pages around inside a PDF — put a page first, reverse a scan that came out back to front,
      or shuffle a few into the order they should have been in. The rearranging happens in this
      browser, on this computer: the file is not uploaded, because Not Only PDF has no server that
      could receive it.
    </p>
  </div>

  {
    /*
    NO `client:*` DIRECTIVE. Astro's hydration directives bootstrap an island with an INLINE
    script, and `script-src 'self'` carries no `'unsafe-inline'` and no nonce (ADR 0014) — so
    the browser refuses the bootstrap, the island never hydrates, and the tool renders as dead
    markup with nothing visible but a CSP line in the console. Measured on `/merge-pdf`; the
    same wall, the same answer on every page since.

    The ENGINES are still not loaded here. The tool starts a worker the first time a file is
    chosen (ADR 0018). Mounting the component is a few kilobytes; starting the engines is
    about 420 KB over the wire (1.8 MB uncompressed) --- it was 6.8 MB until spike 0004 took
    PDFium out of the payload.
  */
  }
  <div id="reorder-tool"></div>

  <script>
    import { type Component, mount } from "svelte";

    import ReorderTool from "../components/ReorderTool.svelte";

    const target = document.getElementById("reorder-tool");
    if (target) {
      // The cast is Astro's type, not ours: `@astrojs/svelte` declares a `.svelte` default
      // export as an ASTRO component, because that is what a `client:*` directive would need.
      // Mounting it by hand is the same module reached through Svelte's own API.
      mount(ReorderTool as unknown as Component, { target });
    }
  </script>

  <noscript>
    <div class="read no-js">
      <h2>This tool needs JavaScript</h2>
      <p>
        The rearranging is done by code running in your browser, so with JavaScript switched off
        there is nothing to do the work. There is no server-side version to fall back to — that is
        the whole design, not a missing feature.
      </p>
      <p>
        With JavaScript on, this page lets you choose a PDF, type the order you want its pages in,
        and download the result. Nothing is sent anywhere.
      </p>
    </div>
  </noscript>

  <div class="read">
    <h2>Saying what order you want</h2>

    <p>
      Type the pages you want moved. <strong>They come first, in the order you type them</strong>,
      and every page you do not type keeps its current order after them. So on a ten-page document,
      <code>3</code> puts page 3 first and leaves 1, 2, 4, 5… following it; <code>4, 2</code> puts those
      two at the front in that order.
    </p>

    <p>
      Ranges work, and they can run backwards: <code>2-5</code> moves those four to the front in order,
      and <code>10-1</code> reverses the whole document — which is what the
      <strong>Reverse the order</strong> button types for you.
    </p>

    <p>
      The page shows the order your pages will come out in before you run anything. That is there
      because the rule above is a rule rather than a guess, and the way to keep it one is to let you
      see the result rather than take our word for it.
    </p>

    {
      STRIP_WITHDRAWN ? (
        <p>
          <strong>{WITHDRAWN_MARK}, and there are none on the other pages either.</strong>{" "}
          <a href="/split-pdf">Split</a> and <a href="/rotate-pdf">rotate</a> showed them briefly
          and had them withdrawn: drawing a page needs a second PDF engine in the same tab as the
          one doing the work, and holding both could cost you the tab mid-operation. Every tool here
          selects pages by number, which is how all three worked before any pictures existed.
          Reordering has a second reason of its own, below, and it has not changed.
        </p>
      ) : (
        <p>
          <strong>There are no page pictures here yet.</strong> <a href="/split-pdf">Split</a> and{" "}
          <a href="/rotate-pdf">rotate</a> show them; reordering does not, and that is a decision
          rather than an oversight. Those two pages let you point at a page and act on it.
          Reordering means saying where a page should <em>go</em>, and a strip you can only look at
          would show you the problem without helping you solve it — so the pictures arrive here with
          dragging, or not at all. The ceiling that held all three back has been built and measured;
          what is left is the interaction, and it is being worked on.
        </p>
      )
    }

    <details>
      <summary><h2>What happens to large files</h2></summary>

      <p>
        Everything is held in this browser tab's memory, so the practical limit is what your device
        can spare rather than an upload size. Not Only PDF refuses a file larger than 512 MB before
        it opens it, and a document of more than 10,000 pages before it moves any of them — so an
        oversized job is refused rather than failing part way through and leaving you with something
        incomplete.
      </p>

      <p>
        What that does not save you from, said here rather than discovered: a very large document
        that is under both limits can still make the tab slow while it runs, and on a phone the
        browser may stop the tab entirely. That is the device's memory ceiling, reached inside the
        PDF engine where nothing can intervene.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary><h2>What is kept, and what is not</h2></summary>

      <p>
        <strong>Every page, and everything attached to the document.</strong> Reordering moves pages around;
        it does not remove any. Bookmarks, attachments, form fields and annotations all come through,
        and what each page displays is unchanged — including pages that inherit their rotation or their
        size from the document rather than carrying it themselves, which is the case most likely to go
        wrong and the one Not Only PDF's tests check directly.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>The document's internal page tree is rebuilt.</strong> PDFs store pages in a tree, and
        moving a page makes the PDF engine flatten that tree into a single list. Nothing a reader shows
        you changes, and no page is lost — but the file's internal structure genuinely differs from the
        original, so a byte-for-byte comparison of the two will not match. That matters to nobody reading
        the document and to anybody comparing its bytes, which is why it is written here rather than left
        to be discovered.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>A signature does not survive.</strong> No tool here preserves one — any change to a PDF
        invalidates a digital signature — but it is worth saying on this page in particular, because "the
        pages are the same pages" is true and the file is still not the same file.
      </p>

      <p>
        A file that is encrypted, damaged, or not a PDF is refused. So is an order that would not
        give every page back exactly once: type a page twice and Not Only PDF says so rather than
        handing you a document with one page duplicated and another gone.
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* The no-JS block is ordinary content, but it only ever appears in place of the tool, so it
     gets the same top margin the tool has. */
  .no-js {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-6);
  }
</style>
